#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536" :question/problem "Consider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/495ed29859c0480fc4675749b905127c.png)\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C$:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$.\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B$, the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x > 0$, while $\\mathbf F$ works against the particle for $x < 0$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/8ca5f3b488d1d892375165229cbb38de.png)\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x < 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0$. So,\n$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C$, then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/ec52fbac8f64b7f821405c9c4ace17e3.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/6d2a5b63bbded94ec826804a33c269bd.png)\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C$:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$.\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B$, it neither helps nor hinders its motion.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/bf8dc9254c2a07c97e07ac05b1ea45ee.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C$, then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/0a16d3a986e8206e4f25db5b3c47d877.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a924e1dd-00ca-578e-b505-4e3f03221f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177701} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8df1f7-183b-5c3a-8cc3-e205bf938b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177702} {:answer/id #uuid "b1116716-bfff-5162-8c94-6241cbb228e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177703} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5be91a-df46-58ab-ad37-c8a848dbb866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177704} {:answer/id #uuid "56ac9579-9e79-5bc9-bf79-797d839e5402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5be91a-df46-58ab-ad37-c8a848dbb866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177704} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da13fc0-c4b2-5e91-b50b-54363d2c1576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177700}] :question/id #uuid "58d69dfd-31b1-5851-b7cd-9ea022ec672e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125380" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3694/Source/Images/q-125380.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∫_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $∫_{C}F \\cdot dr = 0$\n3. $∫_{-C}F \\cdot dr = 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5b3d028-6902-5cd4-be86-6d5c8e3f16c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177731} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a5759-39da-57f4-9128-b36d32f5f51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177732} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8344b7-93aa-58d6-9b3b-f382fe3301c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177733} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd49578-3452-5468-93cc-89e16b62be07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177734} {:answer/id #uuid "66ea681c-9fad-5985-a9fc-8ff39df7f822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ea681c-9fad-5985-a9fc-8ff39df7f822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177735} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a12c16-35e7-5e65-853a-00acabb9e109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177730}] :question/id #uuid "678d2be6-52f5-